{"availability":"available","full":"reata/sqllineage","name":"sqllineage","description":"SQL Lineage Analysis Tool powered by Python","category":"Developer tools","language":"Python","stars":1679,"forks":284,"created":"2019-05-21T14:11:05Z","updated":"2026-10-09T12:52:41Z","license":"MIT","branch":"master","homepage":"","topics":["data-discovery","data-governance","data-lineage","lineage","metadata","sql"],"demo":"https://reata.github.io/sqllineage/","color":"#323057","ink":"#d6caff","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-09T23:11:43.029Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-09T23:11:43.029Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-09T23:11:43.029Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-09T23:11:43.029Z","stars":1679,"forks":284}],"technologies":["Python"],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Never get the hang of a SQL parser? SQLLineage comes to the rescue. Given a SQL command, SQLLineage will tell you its source and target tables, without worrying about Tokens, Keyword, Identifier and all the jagons used by SQL parsers.","Behind the scene, SQLLineage leverages pluggable parser libraries (sqlfluff and sqlparse) to parse the SQL command and analyze the AST. The lineage information is then stored in a graph maintained by a pluggable graph operator with two interchangeable implementations: the networkx-based one, which is the default, and an opt-in rustworkx-based one."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/reata/sqllineage/blob/master/README.md","fingerprint":"ef97686efead066ecb49a35b1b95198aff0eda6d460b21a7422d10aee3906401","extractedAt":"2026-10-09T23:11:43.127Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-09T23:11:43.127Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"Never get the hang of a SQL parser? SQLLineage comes to the rescue. Given a SQL command, SQLLineage will tell you its source and target tables, without worrying about Tokens, Keyword, Identifier and all the jagons used by SQL parsers.","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/reata/sqllineage/blob/master/README.md"},"audience":[],"requir